      "solution_text": "$\\log_{3}729$\n$=\\log_{3}3^{6}$\n$=6$"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "question_text": "Evaluate $\\log_{10}100000$.",
      "structure": "evaluate-power-of-10",
      "mastery": false,
      "options": [
        { "text": "$5$", "correct": true },
        { "text": "$6$", "why": "Miscounted the zeros, treating $100000$ as one million." },
        { "text": "$4$", "why": "Miscounted the zeros, treating $100000$ as ten thousand." },
        { "text": "$100000$", "why": "Restated the argument instead of the exponent of $10$." }
      ],
      "solution_text": "$\\log_{10}100000$\n$=\\log_{10}10^{5}$\n$=5$"
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "question_text": "Evaluate $\\log_{2}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)$.",
      "structure": "evaluate-reciprocal",
      "mastery": false,
      "options": [
        { "text": "$-4$", "correct": true },
        { "text": "$4$", "why": "Dropped the negative sign that comes from the reciprocal $\\frac{1}{16}=2^{-4}$." },
        { "text": "$-16$", "why": "Used the argument $16$ as the exponent instead of writing it as $2^{4}$." },
        { "text": "$-5$", "why": "Used $2^{-5}=\\frac{1}{32}$ instead of $2^{-4}=\\frac{1}{16}$." }
      ],
      "solution_text": "$\\log_{2}\\frac{1}{16}$\n$=\\log_{2}2^{-4}$\n$=-4$"
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "question_text": "Evaluate $\\log_{3}\\sqrt{27}$.",
      "structure": "evaluate-root",
      "mastery": false,
      "options": [
        { "text": "$\\frac{3}{2}$", "correct": true },
        { "text": "$3$", "why": "Took $\\log_{3}27=3$ and ignored the square root." },
        { "text": "$\\frac{1}{2}$", "why": "Used $\\sqrt{3}=3^{\\frac{1}{2}}$ instead of $\\sqrt{27}=3^{\\frac{3}{2}}$." },
        { "text": "$6$", "why": "Doubled the index instead of halving it for the square root." }
      ],
      "solution_text": "$\\log_{3}\\sqrt{27}$\n$=\\log_{3}27^{\\frac{1}{2}}$\n$=\\log_{3}(3^{3})^{\\frac{1}{2}}$\n$=\\log_{3}3^{\\frac{3}{2}}$\n$=\\frac{3}{2}$"
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "question_text": "Evaluate $\\log_{10}0.0001$.",
      "structure": "evaluate-decimal",
      "mastery": false,
      "options": [
        { "text": "$-4$", "correct": true },
        { "text": "$4$", "why": "Ignored that $0.0001$ is less than $1$, so the index should be negative." },
        { "text": "$-3$", "why": "Counted one decimal place too few, treating it as $0.001$." },
        { "text": "$-5$", "why": "Counted one decimal place too many, treating it as $0.00001$." }
      ],
      "solution_text": "$\\log_{10}0.0001$\n$=\\log_{10}10^{-4}$\n$=-4$"
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "question_text": "Evaluate $\\log_{2}40-\\log_{2}5$.",
      "structure": "combine-quotient-to-number",
      "mastery": false,
      "options": [
        { "text": "$3$", "correct": true },
        { "text": "$8$", "why": "Computed $40\\div5=8$ but did not then take $\\log_{2}8$." },
        { "text": "$35$", "why": "Subtracted the arguments $40-5$ instead of dividing them." }
      ],
      "solution_text": "$\\log_{2}40-\\log_{2}5$\n$=\\log_{2}\\frac{40}{5}$\n$=\\log_{2}8$\n$=3$"
    },
    {
      "id": "q8",
      "question_text": "Evaluate $\\log_{2}48+\\log_{2}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$.",
      "structure": "combine-reciprocal-to-number",
      "mastery": true,
      "options": [
        { "text": "$4$", "correct": true },
        { "text": "$16$", "why": "Found $48\\times\\frac{1}{3}=16$ but stopped before taking $\\log_{2}16$." },
        { "text": "$-4$", "why": "Treated $\\log_{2}\\frac{1}{3}$ as making the whole result negative." },
        { "text": "$5$", "why": "Read $16$ as $2^{5}=32$ when finding the index." }
      ],
      "solution_text": "$\\log_{2}48+\\log_{2}\\frac{1}{3}$\n$=\\log_{2}\\left(48\\times\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$\n$=\\log_{2}16$\n$=4$"
    }
  ]
